1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,400
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our oral medicine series.

2
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:14,580
 This video will be about COPD and asthma.

3
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:21,880
 These are two of the main pulmonary diseases that appear on the board exam.

4
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:28,800
 So COPD stands for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, chronic referring to the fact that

5
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:36,580
 it's long term actually causes irreversible damage, obstructive meaning blocking pulmonary

6
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:38,700
 referring to airflow.

7
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:45,540
 So altogether, it's the term for a preventable respiratory disorder that involves the restriction

8
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:50,220
 of airflow that is not fully reversible.

9
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:55,100
 It's currently the third leading cause of death in the United States and the most common

10
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,180
 cause for it is cigarette smoking.

11
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:03,700
 So there are two main clinical manifestations of this disorder.

12
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:09,220
 On the left, we have chronic bronchitis and on the right, we have emphysema.

13
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:14,900
 So chronic bronchitis is all about chronic inflammation in the airways.

14
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:22,020
 So cigarette smoke or environmental pollutants irritate the bronchial walls and increase the

15
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,660
 size of mucus glands and goblet cells.

16
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:31,900
 So this causes lots and lots of mucus production on top of the airways getting inflamed and

17
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:32,900
 narrowed.

18
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:39,540
 So it's a lot harder for that patient to breathe both in and out.

19
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:45,700
 So the classic chronic bronchitis patient is known as a blue bloater.

20
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:51,460
 And they have this chronic productive cough with lots of sputum.

21
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:57,500
 They have a noisy chest with ronkai, which refers to this rattling sound and also they

22
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:00,020
 might have some wheezing sounds.

23
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:07,620
 The chest extra actually appears normal, but they have elevated hemoglobin, peripheral edema,

24
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:14,740
 and the patients tend to be cyanotic with blue skin and overweight, hence the blue bloater

25
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:16,740
 moniker.

26
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:23,140
 Now on the right, we have emphysema, which involves only the alveoli or the air sacs,

27
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:30,540
 which are the functional units of the lung distal to or after the terminal bronchioles.

28
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:38,340
 So chronic smoke inhalation damages the lung epithelium, releasing these inflammatory mediators

29
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:47,500
 and enzymes like elastase that end up destroying lung tissue, resulting in these enlarged air

30
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:51,140
 spaces or enlarged alveoli.

31
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:59,660
 So they also lose elastic recoil as a result of these enzymes like elastase.

32
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:06,140
 So this makes breathing a lot more difficult, specifically breathing out or expiration.

33
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:12,980
 So how you can remember this, whereas chronic bronchitis affects both inspiration and expiration.

34
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:20,020
 Emphysema affects more expiration, so E for emphysema, E for expiration.

35
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:24,340
 The classic emphysema patient is known as a pink puffer.

36
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:29,900
 So they usually don't have a cough or much sputum, and they have a pretty quiet chest,

37
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:31,820
 no ronkai or wheezing.

38
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:38,300
 However, their chest is often barrel shaped with these over-inflated lungs due to the

39
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:43,100
 trapping of air, because they really struggle to expiry that air.

40
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:50,220
 So as a result, the diaphragm is usually flattened, and the heart is usually smaller, because

41
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:54,500
 it's squished between those oversized lungs.

42
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:59,900
 And then the puffer part comes from having smoked for a long period of time.

43
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:07,700
 So pink puffers tend to be old, thin, and have severe dyspnea or trouble breathing.

44
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:13,980
 Now most patients with COPD may exhibit features of both of these diseases, so you could fall

45
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:20,020
 into one category or the other, or you could exhibit symptoms and signs from both of these

46
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:24,220
 unique categories.

47
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:28,340
 So how about diagnosis of COPD?

48
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:29,820
 Well we have a couple of methods.

49
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:37,540
 The spirometer is used to measure the amount of air that a person can breathe out.

50
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:43,260
 So how this works is the patient would take a deep breath and blow into this tube as hard

51
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:49,060
 as possible with a clip on their nose, so all the air is being released through the mouth.

52
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:55,020
 And it's hooked up to a machine that records the results.

53
00:04:55,020 --> 00:05:05,940
 And so the forced expiry volume in one second, that's FEV1, that is less than 70% the predicted

54
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:13,780
 volume called the forced vital capacity, or FVC for short, in the absence of any other

55
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:19,740
 pulmonary disease is given a COPD diagnosis.

56
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:26,500
 Now depending on the severity, COPD is labeled anywhere from stage one for mild to stage

57
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:30,020
 four for very severe.

58
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:36,540
 We can also do pulse oximetry, so pulse oximeter is a lot easier to do, and it measures the

59
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:44,860
 oxygen saturation or the percentage of hemoglobin saturated with oxygen in the arterial blood.

60
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:50,740
 So it isn't used to diagnose COPD, but it can be used to supplement a diagnosis, or in

61
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:55,900
 our case, help determine the patient's current health status and the pulmonary function

62
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:58,380
 while in the dental chair.

63
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:04,820
 So the spirometer is something that a medical professional would be involved in to diagnose

64
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:05,980
 the patient.

65
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:10,260
 The pulse oximeter is just something that we could use in the dental chair to monitor

66
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:13,100
 their current status.

67
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:22,540
 Mental blood gas measurement and chest radiographs can also aid in the diagnosis of COPD.

68
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:29,220
 Now although COPD is irreversible, medications can be used in order to slow the progression

69
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:32,220
 or at least manage the disease.

70
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:40,180
 So inhaled anticholinergics are considered bronchodilators, and they function to reduce

71
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:46,940
 mucous secretion in the airways and relax smooth muscle in order to open up the airways

72
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:51,820
 by blocking acetylcholine at the muscarinic receptors.

73
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:57,620
 So some examples would be iprotropium and teotropium, the tropiums.

74
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:07,180
 Now next we have the inhaled beta adrenergic agonists, and these are also called bronchodilators.

75
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:13,100
 These are also considered bronchodilators, and they function to relax smooth muscle of

76
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:18,260
 the airways by increasing cyclic AMP levels.

77
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:25,740
 Some are fast acting like epinephrine, while others are longer acting like endocateral.

78
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:32,580
 Combining these two categories can have significant benefits because they work by different mechanisms

79
00:07:32,580 --> 00:07:35,860
 but they work to achieve the same outcome.

80
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:44,020
 They both function to open the airways essentially and make breathing easier for the COPD patients.

81
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:50,580
 Corticosteroids work by reducing inflammation and suppressing the immune response.

82
00:07:50,580 --> 00:07:55,200
 Some examples are fluticazone, which is an inhaled drug and prednisone, which is an

83
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:57,380
 oral medication.

84
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:02,780
 And lastly we have the phosphodiesterase inhibitors, like theophiline, as sort of a

85
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:08,860
 last resort, this medication can have some side effects that are not so fun.

86
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:15,900
 Now I like these drug categories because they're as easy to remember as A, B, C, D.

87
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:20,060
 And so that's how I tend to remember these drug categories.

88
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:26,540
 And one possible management strategy is to start with an anticholinergic for stage 1

89
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:35,740
 COPD and then adding a long-acting bronchodilator if that patient's stage 2, adding a corticosteroid

90
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:43,580
 to the mix if they're stage 3, and then using theophiline as a last resort for stage 4,

91
00:08:43,580 --> 00:08:45,780
 very severe COPD.

92
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:51,260
 So not only do we have this order, A, B, C, D, but it also tends to be the order in which

93
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:55,380
 those drugs are prescribed for COPD patients.

94
00:08:55,380 --> 00:09:00,700
 So a nice little memory tip to help you remember those drug categories.

95
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:07,940
 Next let's talk about some considerations for COPD patients that are in the dental chair.

96
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:13,660
 So smoking cessation is the most effective and cost-effective intervention that can help

97
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:19,740
 manage COPD, so we want to encourage that for patients who are actively smoking.

98
00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:27,260
 Some innervational therapies include things like nicotine replacement and bupropion therapy.

99
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:35,260
 We want to avoid pulmonary irritants, so excellent isolation and suction is really useful for

100
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:38,460
 these types of patients.

101
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:44,420
 Patients with moderate to severe COPD should be placed in a semisupine or upright chair

102
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:51,180
 position instead of a supine position to help prevent difficulty breathing and discomfort

103
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:54,140
 for the patient.

104
00:09:54,140 --> 00:10:00,420
 Bilateral mandibular blocks or bilateral greater palatine blocks can cause an unpleasant airway

105
00:10:00,420 --> 00:10:07,140
 constriction feeling in some of these patients, so that should be avoided whenever possible.

106
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:12,900
 Rubber dams can also constrict breathing ability for patients with severe disease, so avoid

107
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:16,340
 those also when possible.

108
00:10:16,340 --> 00:10:22,900
 We certainly want to avoid narcotics and barbiturates because they are respiratory depressants and

109
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:27,340
 we do not want to be depressing pulmonary function.

110
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:33,540
 We want to avoid macrolide antibiotics like erythromycin and also siprofloxacin if the

111
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:41,100
 patient is taking theofiline because these antibiotics can reduce the metabolism of theofiline

112
00:10:41,100 --> 00:10:48,820
 resulting in theofiline toxicity which may involve things like nausea, vomiting, insomnia,

113
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:52,700
 headache, arrhythmia and even convulsions.

114
00:10:52,700 --> 00:11:01,140
 A pulse oximeter should be used to monitor oxygen saturation throughout the dental procedure.

115
00:11:01,140 --> 00:11:07,580
 Nitrous oxide inhalation generally should not be used for severe COPD patients because nitrous

116
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:14,700
 oxide might accumulate in the air spaces of the compromised lung.

117
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:20,340
 Low flow oxygen at a rate of 2 liters per minute is recommended if a person's oxygen

118
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:25,900
 saturation is below 95%.

119
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:32,820
 In general anesthesia is not recommended for COPD patients for airway concerns.

120
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:41,140
 Lastly, if the patients taking systemic corticosteroids for COPD, they might require supplementation

121
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:46,420
 for major surgical procedures due to the concern for adrenal suppression.

122
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:51,740
 This is a consideration for anyone who takes corticosteroids chronically and we'll talk

123
00:11:51,740 --> 00:11:57,420
 more about that in the next video in this series.

124
00:11:57,420 --> 00:12:03,180
 Now for a poorly controlled or unstable COPD patient, this is somebody who's symptomatic

125
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:10,060
 with shortness of breath at rest, productive cough, upper respiratory infection or an oxygen

126
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:13,220
 saturation that's below 91%.

127
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:16,540
 Those are things that we're considering unstable.

128
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:21,740
 And that type of patient should be rescheduled and the appropriate medical referral should

129
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:22,980
 be made.

130
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:26,220
 It's what I call defer and refer.

131
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:32,580
 So as in the past, if the patient needs emergency care, you can consider some conservative treatment

132
00:12:32,580 --> 00:12:38,820
 or a hospital referral depending on the presence or absence of symptoms, your own emergency

133
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:41,940
 preparedness and a multitude of other factors.

134
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:48,420
 But for the board exam, if a situation is unstable, it's uncontrolled, it's symptomatic,

135
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:52,900
 then we want to refer for medical consultation.

136
00:12:52,900 --> 00:12:58,340
 Now also for a COPD patient, we want to look out for cardiovascular comorbidities which

137
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:06,060
 are very common in COPD patients and then follow those patient considerations when necessary.

138
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:12,940
 As far as oral manifestations of COPD, we see things like halitosis, staining of the teeth,

139
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:19,540
 nicotine stomatitis, periodontal disease and oral cancer that are related to smoking because

140
00:13:19,540 --> 00:13:24,460
 the vast majority of these patients are going to be chronic smokers.

141
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:29,780
 Poor oral hygiene might lead to aspiration pneumonia in these patients.

142
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:36,060
 The pneumonia is an infection of the lungs and/or airways and aspiration pneumonia occurs when

143
00:13:36,060 --> 00:13:41,820
 something containing bacteria like food or even saliva is breathed into the lungs or

144
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:45,940
 airways leading to this pneumonia.

145
00:13:45,940 --> 00:13:51,020
 Dry mouth is something we see in a lot of these conditions that we've talked about so

146
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:58,340
 far in the series, but in this case specifically, it's due to the side effects of the anti-colonergic

147
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:01,260
 medications, those tropiums.

148
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:06,860
 And then Steven Johnson syndrome has been linked to theophiling use.

149
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:12,580
 All right, so the second half of this video is going to be about asthma.

150
00:14:12,580 --> 00:14:17,740
 Asthma is also a chronic respiratory disease associated with airway obstruction, but this

151
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:24,820
 one is characterized by reversible episodes of increased airway responsiveness that leads

152
00:14:24,820 --> 00:14:31,460
 to episodes of breathing difficulty, coughing, or wheezing.

153
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:36,980
 And the broccular tissue is sensitive to several stimuli, and there are many things that can

154
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:43,900
 trigger an asthma attack or flare up, it could be, an allergen like pollen, an upper respiratory

155
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:52,860
 infection, exercise, cold air, NSAIDs, chemicals, smoke, and even anxiety and stress.

156
00:14:52,860 --> 00:14:58,420
 And one of the first things you should ask an asthmatic patient is what triggers their

157
00:14:58,420 --> 00:15:05,420
 attacks, and then make sure to avoid those things in the dental setting as much as possible.

158
00:15:05,420 --> 00:15:11,660
 As far as pathophysiology for this one, airway obstruction can be the result of many different

159
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:12,660
 things.

160
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:19,900
 It could be smooth muscle spasm, inflammation of the bronchial mucosa, and/or goblet cell

161
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:26,740
 hyperplasia, that means too many goblet cells, which is also seen in chronic bronchitis.

162
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:33,500
 And the excess mucus from these goblet cells would plug up the airways, making this volume

163
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:35,740
 a lot smaller.

164
00:15:35,740 --> 00:15:42,420
 And as far as diagnosing, as with COPD, spirometry is the gold standard.

165
00:15:42,420 --> 00:15:49,420
 Allergy testing and sputum smears could also be done in addition to that.

166
00:15:49,420 --> 00:15:54,740
 So now for asthma medications, there is a lot of overlap with the COPD ones, and I have

167
00:15:54,740 --> 00:16:00,420
 another ABCD, but this one comes with some caveats.

168
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:09,140
 So anti-asmatics for asthma management are used either for long-term control or short-term

169
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:17,340
 immediate relief, and the combination of those two is usually used to improve lung function.

170
00:16:17,340 --> 00:16:23,340
 And I actually want to start this one with the C, the corticosteroids, because inhaled

171
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:30,940
 corticosteroids are currently the most effective anti-inflammatory medication for treating persistent

172
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:31,940
 asthma.

173
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:37,400
 And similar with COPD, they reduce inflammation.

174
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:43,500
 So some examples are a prednisone, budessinide, and fluticazone.

175
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:50,860
 Now we also have our bronchodilators back once again, these beta-adronergic agonists, and

176
00:16:50,860 --> 00:16:56,460
 they activate the beta-2 receptors to relax smooth muscle of the airways just like they

177
00:16:56,460 --> 00:16:59,660
 did with COPD.

178
00:16:59,660 --> 00:17:07,460
 For an acute asthma attack, the first line of defense is going to be a short-acting bronchodilator,

179
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:12,820
 like albuterol, via an inhaler, or a nebulizer.

180
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:18,860
 You can also pair this with supplemental oxygen via a face mask or nasal hood, and if the

181
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:26,020
 symptoms worsen or do not improve after that, then you would administer epinephrine subcutaneously

182
00:17:26,020 --> 00:17:31,580
 and likely activate EMS, aka call 911.

183
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:37,980
 What's cool is that there are combination drugs that combine a corticosteroid with a long-acting

184
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:40,340
 beta-adronergic agonist.

185
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:51,900
 For example, 4-motorol and budessinide combined become cymbicort, or salmetarol, and fluticazone

186
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:56,860
 combined become adver, and you may have heard of those names before, and that's what's

187
00:17:56,860 --> 00:18:00,620
 going on with those drugs.

188
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:07,340
 Let's go to our A for antihistamines, and those block the histamine receptor.

189
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:13,080
 Cymbine is an organic compound released by mast cells during degranulation, which happens

190
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:21,280
 when an allergen binds to the IgE, the immunoglobulin E, attached to those mast cells, and histamine

191
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:29,420
 is responsible for three things in the classic allergic response, redness, swelling, and itchiness,

192
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:36,900
 so blocking that with diphenhydramine or benedryl, or phexofenidine, which is a legra, and you

193
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:41,500
 may have heard of those two before, helps to calm the allergic response.

194
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:48,100
 Now, there is an asterisk after antihistamines, because antihistamines are not prescribed

195
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:53,020
 for asthma, they're given for allergies.

196
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:58,740
 But if allergies trigger your asthma, then that's when an antihistamine can be used to

197
00:18:58,740 --> 00:19:05,140
 help control asthma and prevent those asthma attacks, so hopefully that makes sense.

198
00:19:05,140 --> 00:19:12,300
 Decongestants, or D, do exactly what you'd expect, they reduce nasal congestion.

199
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:20,020
 And pseudo-ephedrin, brand name pseudofed, is an A1 or alpha-1 selective agonist, which

200
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:26,760
 means it's a vasoconstrictor, so it constricts nasal blood vessels, reducing nasal swelling

201
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:28,580
 and inflammation.

202
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:36,260
 It's mostly used to treat cold, flu, allergies, and sinusitis, but once again, if allergies

203
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:43,940
 are the precipitating factor for asthma, it can be used to indirectly manage your asthma.

204
00:19:43,940 --> 00:19:50,060
 And lastly, we have leukotrying receptor antagonists, which of course, block leukotrying, which

205
00:19:50,060 --> 00:19:59,140
 is a pro-inflammatory mediator that the body releases after coming in contact with an allergen.

206
00:19:59,140 --> 00:20:07,700
 And these leukasts are examples of the leukotrying receptor antagonists.

207
00:20:07,700 --> 00:20:10,500
 So patient considerations for asthma.

208
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:17,060
 If we see asthma on the patient box, what are things we want to be thinking about?

209
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:22,220
 Well the primary goal is to prevent an acute asthma attack.

210
00:20:22,220 --> 00:20:25,020
 That's the last thing that we want in the dental chair.

211
00:20:25,020 --> 00:20:28,740
 So we want to avoid any precipitating factors.

212
00:20:28,740 --> 00:20:31,460
 So what are those precipitating factors?

213
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:33,660
 Stress and anxiety is one of them.

214
00:20:33,660 --> 00:20:36,620
 So we want to reduce stress, be kind and gentle.

215
00:20:36,620 --> 00:20:43,260
 Again, explain the procedures, answer their questions, and we also could consider a short-acting

216
00:20:43,260 --> 00:20:49,780
 benzodiazepine before the appointment, and we can consider nitrous oxide during the appointment

217
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:54,380
 as long as the patient isn't a symptomatic for asthma at that moment.

218
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:57,820
 They're not actively wheezing or something like that.

219
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:03,620
 We also want to confirm the patient is taking their asthma medication as directed, and we

220
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:12,340
 want to have their rescue inhaler and an EpiPen ready and accessible if they have an attack.

221
00:21:12,340 --> 00:21:17,340
 Once again, we want to have excellent isolation to minimize any tooth enamel, dust, or any

222
00:21:17,340 --> 00:21:21,020
 other pulmonary irritants that could trigger an episode.

223
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:26,700
 We want to avoid NSAIDs because they have been shown to precipitate asthma attacks in

224
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:29,220
 a small percent of the population.

225
00:21:29,220 --> 00:21:31,980
 So that's very, very important to note.

226
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:36,700
 Once again, avoid narcotics and barbiturates because they have respiratory depressive

227
00:21:36,700 --> 00:21:40,900
 properties and can also trigger an attack.

228
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:44,580
 We want to track the patient's oxygen saturation.

229
00:21:44,580 --> 00:21:47,360
 Once again, valuable information to have.

230
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:55,900
 It should be somewhere between 97% and 100%, and if it drops below that 95% level, then

231
00:21:55,900 --> 00:22:01,340
 consider oxygen supplementation, that low-flow oxygen.

232
00:22:01,340 --> 00:22:04,780
 And again, I would reschedule, defer, and refer.

233
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:09,420
 If the patient is actively coughing, wheezing, they forgot to take their medication that they

234
00:22:09,420 --> 00:22:13,180
 usually do, etc.

235
00:22:13,180 --> 00:22:19,260
 Oral manifestations of asthma, the zerostomia, or dry mouth, this time is due to the usage

236
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:27,460
 of inhalers that dry out the mouth, as opposed to the anti-colonergics from COPD, which also

237
00:22:27,460 --> 00:22:30,300
 dry out the mouth.

238
00:22:30,300 --> 00:22:36,700
 These patients have increased carious risk due to the decreased salivary flow.

239
00:22:36,700 --> 00:22:43,180
 Gastroesophageal reflux disease is common in patients with asthma and is exacerbated

240
00:22:43,180 --> 00:22:48,420
 by the use of bronchodilators and theophiline.

241
00:22:48,420 --> 00:22:54,940
 Oral candidiasis occurs in about 5% of patients who used inhaled corticosteroids for long

242
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:57,620
 periods of time.

243
00:22:57,620 --> 00:23:03,460
 Enamel defects have been associated with severe asthma in children, while periodontitis

244
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:08,260
 has been associated with severe asthma in adults.

245
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:14,260
 And asthmatics who are mouth breathers may be associated with higher rates of malocclusion,

246
00:23:14,260 --> 00:23:22,820
 specifically excess overjet, posterior crossbite, high palatal vault, and increased face height,

247
00:23:22,820 --> 00:23:28,820
 all associated with habitual mouth breathing.

248
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:33,780
 And since we're talking about pulmonary diseases, I wanted to briefly cover indications and

249
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:39,900
 contraindications to both nitrous oxide in this slide and oxygen on the next slide,

250
00:23:39,900 --> 00:23:44,020
 the two most used gases in dentistry.

251
00:23:44,020 --> 00:23:50,660
 Nitrous oxide can be helpful for someone with dental anxiety, a strong gag reflex, and what

252
00:23:50,660 --> 00:23:52,980
 we just talked about, asthma.

253
00:23:52,980 --> 00:24:00,260
 And since nitrous oxide is not irritating to the trachea and the bronchi, asthma is not

254
00:24:00,260 --> 00:24:06,900
 a contraindication to the use of nitrous oxide, provided the patient is not having an attack

255
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:10,100
 or wheezing at that moment.

256
00:24:10,100 --> 00:24:18,380
 So poorly controlled asthma we defer and refer, but otherwise nitrous is totally fine.

257
00:24:18,380 --> 00:24:24,660
 But in fact, there's a benefit in administering nitrous oxide, since in many asthmatics, the

258
00:24:24,660 --> 00:24:29,940
 primary precipitate is usually emotional stress, especially in children.

259
00:24:29,940 --> 00:24:35,380
 So you could see how nitrous oxide could actually help prevent us from having an attack in the

260
00:24:35,380 --> 00:24:37,060
 chair.

261
00:24:37,060 --> 00:24:43,940
 Now there are a lot of contraindications, most of which are relative, not absolute contraindications,

262
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:48,220
 but I still want to mention them so you're aware of them when it's time for you to sit

263
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:49,940
 for your board exam.

264
00:24:49,940 --> 00:24:56,420
 So patients with COPD have a reduced ability to move gases into and out of the lungs.

265
00:24:56,420 --> 00:24:59,460
 We talked about that at the very beginning of this video.

266
00:24:59,460 --> 00:25:05,180
 Therefore, it's more difficult to administer nitrous oxide, and more importantly, is more

267
00:25:05,180 --> 00:25:09,380
 difficult to get it out of the patient's lungs.

268
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:16,220
 While it can be used in mild to moderate cases, severe COPD patients have a respiratory drive

269
00:25:16,220 --> 00:25:22,660
 triggered by decreased oxygen levels, as opposed to a healthy patient whose drive to breathe

270
00:25:22,660 --> 00:25:26,820
 is triggered by increased carbon dioxide levels.

271
00:25:26,820 --> 00:25:32,700
 So what that means is administering nitrous oxide, which is always given alongside lots

272
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:39,240
 of oxygen, can actually decrease the patient's drive to breathe, which is potentially very,

273
00:25:39,240 --> 00:25:42,660
 very dangerous for a severe COPD patient.

274
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:44,260
 So we could put a little asterisk here.

275
00:25:44,260 --> 00:25:45,260
 It's not.

276
00:25:45,260 --> 00:25:48,060
 And for mild to moderate COPD patients, it's okay.

277
00:25:48,060 --> 00:25:52,580
 But once we get to that severe and above level, we really want to try to avoid nitrous

278
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:56,060
 oxide for those patients.

279
00:25:56,060 --> 00:25:57,060
 Nasal obstruction.

280
00:25:57,060 --> 00:26:03,940
 So since nitrous oxide must be administered through a nasal mask, this is a relative

281
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:09,420
 contraindication depending on how severe the obstruction is.

282
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:15,780
 Even habitual mouth breathers may have difficulty using the nasal mask.

283
00:26:15,780 --> 00:26:22,020
 Multiple sclerosis involves nerve de-biolination, especially in the central nervous system.

284
00:26:22,020 --> 00:26:28,460
 And leads to weakness, poor muscle coordination, speech disturbances among other neurological

285
00:26:28,460 --> 00:26:29,900
 disturbances.

286
00:26:29,900 --> 00:26:34,580
 And chronic use of nitrous oxide can produce similar symptoms.

287
00:26:34,580 --> 00:26:39,300
 So it's not recommended to use nitrous oxide chronically in these patients.

288
00:26:39,300 --> 00:26:43,620
 One or two times is perfectly fine, though.

289
00:26:43,620 --> 00:26:44,620
 Pregnancy.

290
00:26:44,620 --> 00:26:45,620
 This is an important one.

291
00:26:45,620 --> 00:26:51,860
 Nitrous oxide readily enters fetal circulation, and it can be toxic to cells undergoing

292
00:26:51,860 --> 00:26:53,380
 mitosis.

293
00:26:53,380 --> 00:26:58,900
 So pregnant patients should avoid nitrous oxide, especially when they are early on in

294
00:26:58,900 --> 00:27:02,060
 their pregnancy.

295
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:07,020
 Psychiatric disorders are often managed with prescribed medications, but they can be difficult

296
00:27:07,020 --> 00:27:08,020
 to manage.

297
00:27:08,020 --> 00:27:14,020
 It's probably a good idea to consult the patient's psychiatrist before administering nitrous

298
00:27:14,020 --> 00:27:20,700
 oxide because their reaction to inhalation sedation could be unpredictable.

299
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:25,780
 All with that, communication barriers are important because much of the monitoring of

300
00:27:25,780 --> 00:27:30,900
 a patient's conscious sedation with nitrous oxide is done verbally to make sure it's working

301
00:27:30,900 --> 00:27:31,900
 properly.

302
00:27:31,900 --> 00:27:35,940
 You're checking in with the patient, checking to see how they're feeling, and confirming

303
00:27:35,940 --> 00:27:39,420
 with the patient that they're at the right stage of sedation.

304
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:45,060
 So having a language barrier could be a potential problem.

305
00:27:45,060 --> 00:27:46,580
 Otitis media.

306
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:51,180
 So nitrous oxide should be avoided in children with a middle ear infection.

307
00:27:51,180 --> 00:27:58,460
 That's what otitis media is, because of concerns about pressure buildup and gas diffusion.

308
00:27:58,460 --> 00:28:00,820
 And lastly, sickle cell disease.

309
00:28:00,820 --> 00:28:07,580
 So regular use of nitrous oxide can actually interfere with vitamin B12 metabolism, which

310
00:28:07,580 --> 00:28:13,700
 is a problem because these patients, the sickle cell patients, have a B12 deficiency

311
00:28:13,700 --> 00:28:18,000
 already.

312
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:19,000
 All right.

313
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:24,500
 And lastly, let's talk about a couple indications and contraindications for oxygen use.

314
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:29,820
 So in the yes column, we have COPD and asthma, the two main diseases we talked about.

315
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:37,100
 And humidified low flow oxygen can be provided to COPD patients safely to help alleviate an

316
00:28:37,100 --> 00:28:38,780
 unpleasant airway.

317
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:43,340
 And oxygen is also safe and sometimes indicated for asthma patients.

318
00:28:43,340 --> 00:28:48,500
 We talked about this and those patient consideration slides for both COPD and asthma.

319
00:28:48,500 --> 00:28:50,700
 So that makes perfect sense.

320
00:28:50,700 --> 00:28:57,060
 I want to be clear that once again, there are no absolute contraindications to oxygen,

321
00:28:57,060 --> 00:29:02,140
 but it should be administered with caution in two specific scenarios.

322
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:07,980
 And that's for patients who have taken or are currently taking bleomycin, which is a

323
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:14,900
 chemotherapeutic agent that can damage the lungs and patients who are suffering from

324
00:29:14,900 --> 00:29:19,180
 paraquat poisoning, which is a toxic herbicide.

325
00:29:19,180 --> 00:29:25,500
 And since oxygen is administered alongside nitrous oxide when doing inhalation sedation,

326
00:29:25,500 --> 00:29:33,740
 these two things would also be contraindications to nitrous oxide sedation as well.

327
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:34,740
 All right.

328
00:29:34,740 --> 00:29:35,740
 So that's it for this video guys.

329
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:40,020
 Thank you so much for watching, please like this video if you enjoyed it and subscribe

330
